<p>Lebanese and Israeli envoys began a new round of direct talks in Washington on Thursday, the third such meeting this year as a U.S.-backed ceasefire nears expiration. Diplomats said the talks began just after 9 a.m. at the U.S. State Department and were scheduled to continue Thursday and Friday. A senior Lebanese official said Lebanon would demand that Israel stop its attacks, while Israel has sought guarantees that Hezbollah will be disarmed and that Israeli forces can continue operations in Lebanon until that issue is resolved. The Israeli military said Thursday it was striking Hezbollah targets in southern Lebanon after evacuation warnings, and said a Hezbollah drone fell in Israeli territory, wounding several civilians. Lebanon&#x27;s Health Ministry said 22 people, including eight children, were killed in intensified Israeli airstrikes before the negotiations.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/2FcfDLbwe9KxMAkEeIrEbg32lp6Fy-Ye0Bazv3CpgO5IJHAP9OSvifzHRjsyON14a5usUXH8EwQm62ZE2XaOdnbAkkvwd7ZuBIj6JGfajGBM8VjQOvd60fQD0WtVA7lp5QRXBrthll-itA_7d63qAQho-29OXkrA8OkWFFDa1SqjSFkTuw' alt='Funeral of two civil defense members killed in Israeli strikes, in Sidon, Lebanon, May 13, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Named envoys: Lebanon was represented by envoy Simon Karam and Ambassador Nada Hamadeh, while Israel&#x27;s delegation included Ambassador Yechiel Leiter and deputy national security adviser Yossi Draznin.</li><li>U.S. team: A State Department official said U.S. Ambassador to Lebanon Michel Issa, U.S. Ambassador to Israel Mike Huckabee and department official Mike Needham joined the talks.</li><li>Lebanese politics: Lebanese President Joseph Aoun and Prime Minister Nawaf Salam monitored the Washington negotiations from Baabda Palace and agreed to keep coordinating as the talks proceeded.</li><li>Hezbollah stance: Hezbollah objected to Beirut&#x27;s attendance, and figures close to the group described direct contact with Israel as surrender.</li></ul><